---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../../styles/growth-editorial.css';
const title="How to Plan a China Factory Visit";
const description="Plan supplier appointments, travel time and an achievable factory visit agenda. Prepare the records, people and follow-up your buying decision needs.";
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} crumbs={[{href:"/guides/",label:"Guides"}]} current={title} cta={false}>
<article class="growth-article">
<header><p class="eyebrow">Visit planning</p><h1>How to plan a China factory visit</h1><p class="lead">Start with your product and the decision you need to make. Confirm the supplier, production address and meeting scope before arranging the route.</p></header>
<nav class="jump-links" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#prepare">Before booking</a><a href="#capacity">Factories per day</a><a href="#agenda">Example agenda</a><a href="#follow-up">After the visit</a></nav>
<section id="prepare"><h2>Work through the decisions in order</h2><ol class="planning-steps">
<li><h3>Define what you need to learn</h3><p>Bring a product specification or sample reference, expected quantity and a short list of decisions. Exploring a process, reviewing a sample and negotiating an order need different meetings.</p></li>
<li><h3>Choose suppliers and production locations</h3><p>Use the <a href="/tools/china-manufacturing-hub-finder/">manufacturing hub finder</a> if you are still choosing a region. For an existing shortlist, confirm which company and address actually perform the work relevant to your product.</p></li>
<li><h3>Agree access and participants</h3><p>Get the date, address, host contact, available areas and meeting scope in writing. Ask which technical or commercial staff can attend. Confirm any photography or filming permission separately.</p></li>
<li><h3>Build the route from confirmed appointments</h3><p>Check door-to-door travel, entrance procedures, meal breaks and time for notes. Ask each factory about its production schedule and holiday arrangements; a market opening does not confirm that a production line is operating.</p></li>
<li><h3>Confirm the budget and records</h3><p>Agree support, transport and written deliverables. Use the <a href="/guides/china-factory-visit-cost/">cost planning guide</a> to check exclusions and the <a href="/guides/questions-to-ask-china-factory-visit/">supplier questions</a> to request records before you arrive.</p></li>
</ol></section>
<section id="capacity"><h2>How many factories can you visit in one day?</h2><div class="two-up"><article><h3>One detailed visit</h3><p>Allow a full day when you need a production walk-through, technical discussion, sample review and document follow-up. A first visit or a complex product may need more time than a repeat meeting.</p></article><article><h3>Two focused appointments</h3><p>Consider two only when the sites are close, the purpose of each meeting is narrow and both hosts have confirmed the schedule. Keep time for travel and notes; do not assume two addresses in the same city are nearby.</p></article></div><p>These are planning options, not a daily quota. For a multi-city trip, reserve travel blocks separately. Reduce the number of appointments if a delayed first meeting would make the second impractical.</p></section>
<section id="agenda"><h2>An example agenda for one detailed visit</h2><p>Illustrative time blocks, not a fixed itinerary. Confirm the sequence and access with the host.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Adapt the agenda to your product and appointment</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Block</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th><th scope="col">Record</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Arrival and introductions</th><td>Confirm participants, product scope and permitted areas.</td><td>Names, roles and changes to the plan.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Production review</th><td>Follow the relevant process and identify outsourced steps.</td><td>Observations, permitted photos and questions needing evidence.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Technical and sample discussion</th><td>Compare specifications, samples and quality checks.</td><td>Sample references and unresolved requirements.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Commercial discussion</th><td>Review the quote assumptions, MOQ and schedule.</td><td>Written terms to confirm and responsible contacts.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Close and debrief</th><td>Agree next steps and leave time to write notes.</td><td>Action owner, evidence needed and response date.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></section>
<section id="follow-up" class="note"><h2>Write the comparison before the next meeting</h2><p>Separate what you saw from what the supplier said and what you still need to verify. Keep quote versions and sample references with the notes. Use the same <a href="/tools/factory-visit-scorecard/">supplier comparison template</a> for each company.</p><p>Where a formal audit, testing or shipment inspection is needed, arrange that as a separate task. A hosted visit does not provide those conclusions.</p></section>
<div class="related"><a href="/guides/china-factory-visit-checklist/">On-site checklist</a><a href="/services/factory-visit-planning/">Get help arranging the route</a></div></article>
</BaseLayout>
